<body>

<h1>SSO Login API &#8212; Integration Guide</h1>
<p>This guide explains how your application signs an employee in to the AKOS MD patient portal
without asking them for a separate username or password. Your system authenticates the employee,
then calls one API with your issued credentials and the employee's details. The API returns an
access token that represents that employee on the portal.</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Audience:</b> the development team integrating with AKOS MD</li>
<li><b>Protocol:</b> HTTPS &#183; JSON</li>
<li><b>Endpoint:</b> <code>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login</code></li>
<li><b>Version:</b> 1.0</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2>Table of contents</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#overview">How it works</a></li>
<li><a href="#credentials">Credentials you receive</a></li>
<li><a href="#endpoint">Endpoint</a></li>
<li><a href="#request">Request</a></li>
<li><a href="#response">Success response</a></li>
<li><a href="#token">Using the access token</a></li>
<li><a href="#errors">Error responses</a></li>
<li><a href="#ip">IP whitelisting</a></li>
<li><a href="#samples">Code samples</a></li>
<li><a href="#checklist">Go&#8209;live checklist</a></li>
<li><a href="#practices">Recommended practices</a></li>
<li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
<li><a href="#support">Support</a></li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="overview"></a>How it works</h2>
<ol>
<li>The employee signs in to <b>your</b> application, using whatever method you already use.</li>
<li>Your <b>server</b> calls the SSO login API with your Client ID, your Client Secret, and the
employee's email and name.</li>
<li>AKOS MD verifies your credentials, then finds the employee's portal account &#8212; creating it
automatically the first time that email is seen.</li>
<li>The API returns an access token and the employee's portal profile.</li>
<li>Your application opens the portal for the employee using that token.</li>
</ol>

<blockquote>You are trusted to have authenticated the employee. Never expose your Client Secret to a
browser, mobile app, or any code the employee could inspect &#8212; the call must be made
server&#8209;to&#8209;server.</blockquote>

<h3>Employees never set a portal password</h3>
<p>Accounts created through this API cannot be signed in to with a password on the portal login
screen. Every session must start with a call to this API. If an employee needs a standalone portal
password, they can use the portal's own forgot&#8209;password flow.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="credentials"></a>Credentials you receive</h2>
<p>AKOS MD issues the following to you before integration. Treat all of them as secrets in transit
and store the secret the way you store any other production credential.</p>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>Credential</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Client ID</b></td><td>Identifies the company an employee belongs to. If your organisation has several companies on the portal, you receive one Client ID per company.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Parent Client ID</b></td><td>Issued only when your organisation is a group with multiple companies. It identifies the group and is the account your credentials are validated against.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Client Secret</b></td><td>The shared secret proving the call comes from you. A group receives a single secret that works for every company in the group.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Single company</h3>
<p>You receive a Client ID and a Client Secret. Send both on every call and omit
<code>parent_client_id</code>.</p>

<h3>Group with multiple companies</h3>
<p>You receive a Parent Client ID, one Client ID per company, and a single Client Secret belonging
to the parent. On every call send the Parent Client ID, the Client ID of the company the employee
works for, and the parent's secret. You may also send the Parent Client ID as the Client ID when
the employee works for the parent company itself.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="endpoint"></a>Endpoint</h2>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Method</td><td><code>POST</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Path</td><td><code>/api/auth/sso-client-login</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Full URL</td><td><code>https://&lt;akos-md-host&gt;/api/auth/sso-client-login</code> &#8212; the host is supplied with your credentials</td></tr>
<tr><td>Content type</td><td><code>application/json</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authentication</td><td>None &#8212; your credentials travel in the request body</td></tr>
<tr><td>Encryption</td><td>None at the payload level; transport security is provided by HTTPS</td></tr>
</table>

<blockquote>Some other AKOS MD endpoints expect an encrypted payload. This one does not &#8212; send
and read plain JSON.</blockquote>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="request"></a>Request</h2>

<h3>Body fields</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>parent_client_id</code></td><td>string</td><td>Only for groups</td><td>Your Parent Client ID. Omit it if you were not issued one.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>client_id</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>The Client ID of the company the employee belongs to.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>client_secret</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>Your Client Secret.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>email</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>The employee's email address. This identifies the account, so send the same address every time for the same person.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mobile</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>The employee's mobile number. Recommended &#8212; it is used for portal notifications.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>firstname</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>The employee's first name.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>last_name</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>The employee's last name.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Send only the fields listed above. Any additional field is rejected with a validation error.
<code>first_name</code> is accepted in place of <code>firstname</code>, and <code>lastname</code> or
<code>last-name</code> in place of <code>last_name</code>, if that suits your existing data model
better.</p>

<h3>Example &#8212; group with multiple companies</h3>
<pre>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "parent_client_id": "&lt;your parent client id&gt;",
  "client_id": "&lt;company client id&gt;",
  "client_secret": "&lt;your client secret&gt;",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "mobile": "9876543210",
  "firstname": "John",
  "last_name": "Doe"
}</pre>

<h3>Example &#8212; single company</h3>
<pre>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": "&lt;your client id&gt;",
  "client_secret": "&lt;your client secret&gt;",
  "email": "jane@example.com",
  "mobile": "9876543211",
  "firstname": "Jane",
  "last_name": "Roe"
}</pre>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="response"></a>Success response</h2>
<p><b>HTTP 200</b> &#8212; the employee is signed in. The same response is returned whether the
account was just created or already existed.</p>

<pre>{
  "success": true,
  "message": "User logged in successfully.",
  "data": true,
  "token": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9....",
  "userDetails": [
    {
      "loggedPatientId": 213,
      "uniquePatientId": "992269",
      "first_name": "John",
      "last_name": "Doe",
      "email": "john@example.com",
      "phone": "9876543210",
      "uuid": "6f1c0e2a-6b7d-4e3f-9a11-2c8d5f0b7e44",
      "role": "Patient",
      "isFirstLogin": 1,
      "isProfileCompleted": 0,
      "company_name": "Acme Inc"
    }
  ]
}</pre>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>token</code></td><td>The employee's access token. Valid for <b>24 hours</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>userDetails</code></td><td>An array containing the employee's portal profile.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>loggedPatientId</code></td><td>The employee's portal identifier. Useful to log on your side for support queries.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>isFirstLogin</code></td><td><code>1</code> until the employee has completed their first portal session.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>isProfileCompleted</code></td><td><code>0</code> until the employee has filled in their portal profile. You may want to route them to the profile page when this is <code>0</code>.</td></tr>
</table>

<blockquote>Read <code>userDetails</code> as an array and take the first entry. Do not assume any
field other than those listed will be present, and ignore fields you do not recognise &#8212; more may
be added over time.</blockquote>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="token"></a>Using the access token</h2>
<ul>
<li>Send it on portal API calls as an <code>Authorization</code> header:
<code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>It expires <b>24 hours</b> after it is issued. There is no refresh call &#8212; request a new token
by calling the SSO login API again.</li>
<li>Request a fresh token each time the employee starts a portal session rather than caching one for
its full lifetime.</li>
<li>Never write the token to a shared log, a URL you did not receive from AKOS MD, or anywhere it
could be read by another user.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="errors"></a>Error responses</h2>
<p>Every failure returns the same shape, with the reason in <code>message</code>:</p>
<pre>{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Invalid client secret.",
  "errors": []
}</pre>
<p>Validation failures return the individual problems in the <code>errors</code> array instead:</p>
<pre>{
  "success": false,
  "errors": ["\"client_secret\" is required"]
}</pre>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>HTTP</th><th>Message</th><th>What it means &amp; what to do</th></tr>
<tr><td>400</td><td>Validation messages in <code>errors</code></td><td>A field is missing, malformed, or not recognised. Check the field list above; a common cause is an extra field or a mistyped email.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid parent client id.</td><td>The Parent Client ID is wrong, or that account is no longer active. Confirm the value with AKOS MD.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid client id.</td><td>The Client ID is wrong, or that company is no longer active. Confirm the value with AKOS MD.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid client secret.</td><td>The secret does not match. If your organisation is a group, make sure you are sending the <b>parent's</b> secret and that <code>parent_client_id</code> is included.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>The client id does not belong to the given parent client id.</td><td>The Client ID is not one of the companies in your group. Check the pairing with AKOS MD.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>API access is not enabled for this client.</td><td>SSO has not been switched on for that account yet. Contact AKOS MD.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>Your IP address is not whitelisted for this client.</td><td>The call came from a server address that has not been registered. See <a href="#ip">IP whitelisting</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>This user belongs to another client.</td><td>That email already has a portal account under a different company. Confirm the employee's company, or contact AKOS MD if they have genuinely moved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>400</td><td>Unable to create the user.</td><td>The account could not be created. Retry once; if it persists, contact AKOS MD with the email and timestamp.</td></tr>
<tr><td>404</td><td>No patient found with the provided email.</td><td>The account could not be read back. Retry once; if it persists, contact AKOS MD.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>How to handle errors</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>400</b> &#8212; a bug in your request. Fix the payload; retrying unchanged will not help.</li>
<li><b>401 / 403</b> &#8212; a configuration or credential problem. Do not retry in a loop; surface a
"sign&#8209;in unavailable" message to the employee and raise it with AKOS MD.</li>
<li><b>Network timeout or 5xx</b> &#8212; retry once after a short delay. Repeating the same call is
safe: it will not create a duplicate account.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="ip"></a>IP whitelisting</h2>
<p>IP whitelisting is optional and is agreed during onboarding. When it is enabled for your account,
calls are accepted only from server addresses you have registered with AKOS MD.</p>
<ul>
<li>Send AKOS MD the public outbound IP addresses of every server that will call the API, including
staging and disaster&#8209;recovery hosts.</li>
<li>Tell AKOS MD in advance whenever those addresses change &#8212; an unregistered address is rejected
with <b>403 Your IP address is not whitelisted for this client.</b></li>
<li>If your traffic leaves through a NAT gateway, load balancer, or proxy, register the address that
gateway presents to the internet, not the private address of the application server.</li>
<li>For a group, the whitelist is held at the group level and covers all of its companies.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="samples"></a>Code samples</h2>

<h3>curl</h3>
<pre>curl -X POST https://&lt;akos-md-host&gt;/api/auth/sso-client-login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
        "parent_client_id": "&lt;parent client id&gt;",
        "client_id": "&lt;client id&gt;",
        "client_secret": "&lt;client secret&gt;",
        "email": "john@example.com",
        "mobile": "9876543210",
        "firstname": "John",
        "last_name": "Doe"
      }'</pre>

<h3>Node.js</h3>
<pre>const response = await fetch(`${AKOS_HOST}/api/auth/sso-client-login`, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    parent_client_id: process.env.AKOS_PARENT_CLIENT_ID, // omit for a single company
    client_id: process.env.AKOS_CLIENT_ID,
    client_secret: process.env.AKOS_CLIENT_SECRET,
    email: employee.email,
    mobile: employee.mobile,
    firstname: employee.firstName,
    last_name: employee.lastName,
  }),
});

const result = await response.json();

if (!response.ok) {
  // result.message, or result.errors for a validation failure
  throw new Error(result.message || (result.errors || []).join(', '));
}

const token = result.token;
const profile = result.userDetails[0];</pre>

<h3>PHP</h3>
<pre>$payload = [
    'parent_client_id' =&gt; getenv('AKOS_PARENT_CLIENT_ID'), // omit for a single company
    'client_id'        =&gt; getenv('AKOS_CLIENT_ID'),
    'client_secret'    =&gt; getenv('AKOS_CLIENT_SECRET'),
    'email'            =&gt; $employee['email'],
    'mobile'           =&gt; $employee['mobile'],
    'firstname'        =&gt; $employee['first_name'],
    'last_name'        =&gt; $employee['last_name'],
];

$ch = curl_init(getenv('AKOS_HOST') . '/api/auth/sso-client-login');
curl_setopt_array($ch, [
    CURLOPT_POST           =&gt; true,
    CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER =&gt; true,
    CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER     =&gt; ['Content-Type: application/json'],
    CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS     =&gt; json_encode($payload),
]);

$body   = curl_exec($ch);
$status = curl_getinfo($ch, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE);
curl_close($ch);

$result = json_decode($body, true);

if ($status !== 200) {
    throw new Exception($result['message'] ?? 'SSO login failed');
}

$token   = $result['token'];
$profile = $result['userDetails'][0];</pre>

<h3>C#</h3>
<pre>var payload = new {
    parent_client_id = parentClientId, // omit for a single company
    client_id        = clientId,
    client_secret    = clientSecret,
    email            = employee.Email,
    mobile           = employee.Mobile,
    firstname        = employee.FirstName,
    last_name        = employee.LastName
};

var content  = new StringContent(JsonSerializer.Serialize(payload), Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
var response = await httpClient.PostAsync($"{akosHost}/api/auth/sso-client-login", content);
var body     = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();

if (!response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
    throw new Exception($"SSO login failed: {body}");</pre>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="checklist"></a>Go&#8209;live checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Confirm the AKOS MD host name for testing and for production &#8212; they are different.</li>
<li>Store the Client ID, Parent Client ID (if issued), and Client Secret in your server&#8209;side
configuration or secret store, never in source control.</li>
<li>Confirm the call is made from your <b>server</b>, not from a browser or mobile app.</li>
<li>If IP whitelisting applies, register your production and staging outbound IP addresses.</li>
<li>Verify a first&#8209;time employee: the account is created and a token is returned.</li>
<li>Verify a returning employee: the same email returns a token without creating a second account.</li>
<li>Verify your handling of each error case &#8212; at minimum a wrong secret and a malformed payload.</li>
<li>Confirm your logs never record the Client Secret or the returned token.</li>
<li>For a group, test at least one employee from each company Client ID.</li>
</ol>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="practices"></a>Recommended practices</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Server&#8209;to&#8209;server only.</b> Anyone holding your secret can obtain a token for any
employee in your company.</li>
<li><b>Use HTTPS.</b> The credentials are in the request body; a plain HTTP call would expose them.</li>
<li><b>Send the employee's canonical email.</b> The email identifies the account. If it changes in
your system, tell AKOS MD rather than sending the new address, which would create a second account.</li>
<li><b>Send name and mobile every time.</b> They are used when the account is first created.</li>
<li><b>Do not cache tokens across employees.</b> Each token belongs to one person.</li>
<li><b>Fail closed.</b> If the call fails, show the employee a clear message rather than continuing
into the portal.</li>
<li><b>Rotate the secret periodically</b> and immediately if you suspect exposure. A rotation takes
effect straight away; coordinate the switchover with AKOS MD.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="faq"></a>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h4>Do I need to create employees in advance?</h4>
<p>No. The account is created automatically the first time an employee signs in.</p>

<h4>What happens if I call the API twice for the same employee?</h4>
<p>Nothing is duplicated. The first call creates the account; every later call simply issues a new
token.</p>

<h4>Can an employee use the portal's normal login screen?</h4>
<p>Not with an account created this way &#8212; no password is set. Each session starts from your
application. An employee who wants a standalone password can set one through the portal's
forgot&#8209;password flow.</p>

<h4>How long is the token valid?</h4>
<p>24 hours. Request a new one for each new session rather than storing it.</p>

<h4>Can I look up or update an employee through this API?</h4>
<p>No. This endpoint only signs an employee in. The response includes their current profile.</p>

<h4>An employee changed companies within our group. What do I do?</h4>
<p>Contact AKOS MD. Calling with a different Client ID for an existing email returns
<b>403 This user belongs to another client.</b> &#8212; accounts are not moved automatically, so that a
mistyped Client ID can never reassign someone.</p>

<h4>Is there a rate limit?</h4>
<p>Yes, a general limit applies to the AKOS MD API. Normal sign&#8209;in traffic is well within it. Do
not use this endpoint for bulk operations or retry loops.</p>

<h4>Can we test before go&#8209;live?</h4>
<p>Yes. AKOS MD provides a test host and a separate set of credentials. Production credentials will
not work against the test host, or the reverse.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="support"></a>Support</h2>
<p>When raising an issue, please include:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Client ID (and Parent Client ID) used &#8212; <b>never</b> the Client Secret</li>
<li>The date and time of the call, with the time zone</li>
<li>The HTTP status code and the <code>message</code> or <code>errors</code> returned</li>
<li>The employee email involved</li>
<li>The public IP address the call was made from</li>
<li>Whether the call was against the test or the production host</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
